  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Cube Roots and Perfect Cubes",
    "description": "Group prime factors in threes to extract cube roots and repair numbers so every prime exponent is a multiple of three.",
    "teachingApproach": "Mirror the square-root pairing idea with prime triples, then move through large exact cubes, decimal and fractional cube roots, exponent repair by multiplication and division, cube boundaries and nearest-cube gaps, and finish with the chapter’s cube rationalising-factor identity.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": "Teach cube-root extraction by prime factorisation exactly as in the chapter: factor the number, group equal primes in threes, and take one from each triple. Extend to large exact cubes, fractions and terminating decimals. Teach that perfect-cube prime exponents are multiples of three, then find least multipliers and divisors, the greatest four-digit perfect cube, and the least addition needed to reach the next cube. End with the chapter rationalising-factor pattern for ∛9−∛3+1, using a=∛3 so a²−a+1 multiplied by a+1 becomes a³+1. Check cube-root answers by cubing them.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5, Square Roots and Cube Roots. Approved scope: 32"
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's switch from pairs to triples.\n\nA square root keeps one factor from every pair; a cube root keeps one from every group of three. That single change drives the whole lesson.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Cube-root factorisation uses triples instead of pairs.\n\n2744 = 2³ × 7³\n\nEach prime appears in a complete group of three.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Take one factor from each triple:\n\n∛2744 = 2 × 7 = 14\n\nCheck: 14³ = 2744.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
